  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Question": "What is \\(\\cos(180°-\\alpha)\\) equal to?",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\cos\\alpha\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\sin\\alpha\\)",
    "C": "\\(-\\cos\\alpha\\)",
    "D": "\\(-\\sin\\alpha\\)"
  },
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "The cosine difference identity gives \\[\\cos(180^\\circ-\\alpha)=\\cos180^\\circ\\cos\\alpha+\\sin180^\\circ\\sin\\alpha=-\\cos\\alpha.\\] This holds for every real angle \\(\\alpha\\), so C is correct.\n\nAlternative method 1: Reflect the unit-circle point\n\nFor any real angle \\(\\alpha\\), its unit-circle point is \\((\\cos\\alpha,\\sin\\alpha)\\). Reflection across the \\(y\\)-axis changes its direction angle to \\(180^\\circ-\\alpha\\), up to a whole number of turns, and changes its coordinates to \\((-\\cos\\alpha,\\sin\\alpha)\\). Cosine is the horizontal coordinate, so\n\\[\\cos(180^\\circ-\\alpha)=-\\cos\\alpha.\\]\nThus C is correct for every real \\(\\alpha\\).",
